% ===== =====
\gdef \vect #1{\mathbf{#1}} % abstract vector
\gdef \cvect #1{\boldsymbol{#1}}
\gdef \basis #1#2{\mathcal{#1}_{#2}} % basis of vector space
\gdef \basev #1#2#3{\{\vect{#1}_{#2}\}_{#2=1}^{#3}} % base vector collection
\gdef \cbasev #1#2#3{\{\cvect{#1}^{#2}\}_{#2=1}^{#3}} % dual basis e^i
\gdef \vrep #1#2{[\vect{#1}]_{#2}} % coordinate representation [v]_B
\gdef \rep #1{[\vect{#1}]}
\gdef \mrep #1#2#3{[{#1}]_{#2}^{#3}} % representation [L]_{C,B}
% 
\gdef \iprod #1#2{\langle #1, #2 \rangle} % inner product
\gdef \tran #1{\vect{#1}^{\mkern-1.5mu\mathsf{T}}}
\gdef \mat #1{\mathbf{#1}} % matrix (representation)
\gdef \field #1{\mathbb{#1}} % 
\gdef \xto #1{\xrightarrow{#1}} % arrow with label
\gdef \xfrom #1{\xleftarrow{#1}} % left arrow with label
\gdef \Hom {\operatorname{Hom}} % morphisms between A and B
\gdef \Iso {\operatorname{Iso}}
\gdef \End {\operatorname{End}} % 
\gdef \Aut {\operatorname{Aut}} % 
\gdef \cat #1{\mathsf{#1}} % category symbol: e.g., \cat{Vect}, \cat{Set}
\gdef \Mat {\operatorname{Mat}}
\gdef \Bilin {\operatorname{Bilin}}
\gdef \t {^{\mathsf{T}}}
\gdef \id {\mat{I}} % identity matrix
\gdef \R {\field{R}} % 
\gdef \C {\field{C}} % 
\gdef \ot {\otimes} % tensor product symbol
\gdef \zero {\vect{0}} % 
\gdef \one {\vect{1}} % 
\gdef \idop {\mathrm{id}} % identity morphism
\gdef \comp {\circ} % composition symbol
\gdef \Set {\cat{Set}} % category of sets
\gdef \Vectk {\cat{Vect}_{\field{k}}} % category of vector spaces
\gdef \Vect {\cat{Vect}} % 
% 
\gdef \BaseB {\basis{B}{}}
\gdef \BaseC {\basis{C}{}}
\gdef \BaseBV {\basis{B}{V}}
\gdef \BaseCW {\basis{C}{W}}
\gdef \BaseE {\basis{E}{}}
\gdef \BaseH {\basis{H}{}}
$$&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Picking up from last time, now that we have some understanding of $\Hom(\R,V)$ and $\Hom(V,\R)$, and with the special object of &lt;strong&gt;duality&lt;/strong&gt; in hand, we can finally set out to study the space of linear maps $\Hom(V,W)$. And what kind of connection exists between the matrix space $\Mat(m,n)$ and the space of linear maps? Let&amp;rsquo;s explore this in this chapter!&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>How to Parse (Relatively) Complex C/C++ Types?</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/cpp_note/simple_type_parsing/</link><pubDate>Thu, 15 Jan 2026 19:35:50 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/cpp_note/simple_type_parsing/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/Science.png" alt="Featured image of post How to Parse (Relatively) Complex C/C++ Types?" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I learned about the type distinction between array pointers and pointer arrays from the blog post of a lovely group member &lt;a class="link" href="https://mahiro.ink/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;@0xa7973908&lt;/a&gt;, but such pure notation is really hard to memorize by rote. What&amp;rsquo;s the logic behind it, really? Let&amp;rsquo;s explore~&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This issue&amp;rsquo;s header image is a screenshot from the MV of the song &lt;a class="link" href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1nkB7YQE1z/" title="Bilibili link"
 target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;サイエンス&lt;/a&gt; (Science) by &lt;a class="link" href="https://x.com/mimi_3mi" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;MIMI&lt;/a&gt;. It&amp;rsquo;s really pleasant to listen to, very fitting for us research dogs (). The MV was illustrated by &lt;a class="link" href="https://x.com/bokarokaku" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;3774.&lt;/a&gt;, whose art is adorable and rock-solid&amp;hellip;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Linear Algebra Notes II</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/linear_algebra/la_2/</link><pubDate>Mon, 20 Oct 2025 09:03:01 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/linear_algebra/la_2/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/Alice.jpg" alt="Featured image of post Linear Algebra Notes II" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Picking up where we left off, now that we&amp;rsquo;ve got a rough idea of what a linear space is about, let&amp;rsquo;s take a look at what kinds of connections exist between linear spaces~!&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;For the header image info, please refer to the previous section, thanks~ The featured track is &lt;strong&gt;お茶ガール&lt;/strong&gt;, a duet by &lt;a class="link" href="https://chata.moo.jp/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;&lt;strong&gt;Chata&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a class="link" href="https://www.7uta.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;&lt;strong&gt;nayuta&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, youthful and full of sunshine, it kept me company through my sophomore year of grinding through linear algebra&amp;hellip;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Linear Algebra Notes I</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/linear_algebra/la_1/</link><pubDate>Mon, 20 Oct 2025 09:00:01 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/linear_algebra/la_1/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/Alice.jpg" alt="Featured image of post Linear Algebra Notes I" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I&amp;rsquo;ve always felt that I never fully grasped linear algebra, especially after studying some abstract algebra — I&amp;rsquo;ve come to realize that linear algebra is far more than just simple matrix operations. Seizing the opportunity to learn continuum mechanics, I&amp;rsquo;ll organize what I know and boldly share my understanding of this subject~&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The featured song is a cover by &lt;a class="link" href="https://space.bilibili.com/316381099" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;&lt;strong&gt;Kano&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, included in the album &lt;a class="link" href="https://music.163.com/#/album?id=37542486" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;&lt;strong&gt;one&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; — the song &lt;strong&gt;Calc.&lt;/strong&gt; The original is a classic Vocaloid song I absolutely adore, produced by &lt;a class="link" href="https://lit.link/en/jimmythumb" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;&lt;strong&gt;JimmyThumbP&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and posted on &lt;em&gt;Niconico Douga&lt;/em&gt;, link: &lt;a class="link" href="https://www.nicovideo.jp/watch/sm12050471" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;Hatsune Miku Original Song「Calc.」&lt;/a&gt;. It&amp;rsquo;s probably more of a love song, actually? But Calc. also gives off a vibe of calculation (lol)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>The Product and Coproduct of Vector Spaces</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/product_and_coproduct_of_vector_space/</link><pubDate>Sun, 05 Oct 2025 19:55:20 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/product_and_coproduct_of_vector_space/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/Eyjafjalla.jpg" alt="Featured image of post The Product and Coproduct of Vector Spaces" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;What exactly is the difference between the direct product (product) and direct sum (coproduct) of vector spaces? Where does their distinction originate? This question that troubled me for a long time has finally found its answer today — let&amp;rsquo;s take a look together~&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The selected song is from the album &lt;a class="link" href="https://music.163.com/#/album?id=72378630" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;&lt;strong&gt;two&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; by &lt;a class="link" href="https://space.bilibili.com/316381099" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;&lt;strong&gt;鹿乃&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, which I recently asked a friend to buy from Japan (hasn&amp;rsquo;t arrived yet). It is also a song by my beloved producer &lt;a class="link" href="https://www.nicovideo.jp/user/865371" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;&lt;strong&gt;電ポルP&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (Koyori, also called Utility Pole P, because the song illustrations often feature utility poles), released in 2014 and sung by v flower: &lt;strong&gt;曖昧劣情Lover&lt;/strong&gt;. It&amp;rsquo;s truly wonderful — I really like it. The featured image is an AI-generated picture of the adorable Eyjafjalla, produced by a certain mysterious group-chat friend&amp;rsquo;s friend &lt;strong&gt;派佬&lt;/strong&gt;. I hope you enjoy it~&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>VS Code Might Actually Be an IDE</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/tools_note/vsc_remote/</link><pubDate>Wed, 17 Sep 2025 14:35:38 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/tools_note/vsc_remote/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/%E7%8C%9B%E7%8B%AC%E3%81%8C%E8%A5%B2%E3%81%86.png" alt="Featured image of post VS Code Might Actually Be an IDE" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Although VS Code&lt;sup id="fnref:1"&gt;&lt;a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref"&gt;1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; is technically an editor, it actually feels more like an IDE (Integrated Development Environment) in disguise. And its most powerful feature is arguably its remote capabilities. Today, let&amp;rsquo;s talk about how to configure VS Code into a multi-purpose IDE that rivals Visual Studio&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The song I&amp;rsquo;ve chosen this time is a highly acclaimed Vocaloid track with countless covers, and one that I&amp;rsquo;m personally very fond of: &lt;strong&gt;猛独が襲う&lt;/strong&gt; (Moudoku ga Osou), written and composed by producer &lt;a class="link" href="https://twitter.com/hihumi_v" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;一二三 (Hifumi)&lt;/a&gt;. I happened to hear it at noon and thought it was fantastic, so I went with it. The featured image is from the equally stunning MV for this song, illustrated by &lt;a class="link" href="https://twitter.com/kyu_fu" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;休符 (Kyufu)&lt;/a&gt; — it&amp;rsquo;s really gorgeous~ I also have to point out that the incredible guitar work on this song was performed by &lt;a class="link" href="https://twitter.com/junjun_mimi03" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;じゅみ (Jumi)&lt;/a&gt;. I must immediately confess that the guitar is my favorite part of this song lol&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>(Maybe) a Git Tutorial? Part Three</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/shell_note/git_how/git_3/</link><pubDate>Tue, 26 Aug 2025 18:28:16 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/shell_note/git_how/git_3/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/Tatara-Kogasa.jpg" alt="Featured image of post (Maybe) a Git Tutorial? Part Three" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The final section &amp;ndash; let&amp;rsquo;s talk about collaborating with others on GitHub~&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
