&lt;h2 id="git-a-name-both-familiar-and-strange"&gt;Git, a Name Both Familiar and Strange&amp;hellip;
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Maybe it&amp;rsquo;s just the environment I&amp;rsquo;m in, but a lot of people around me don&amp;rsquo;t know what Git is. They&amp;rsquo;ve all heard of &lt;em&gt;GitHub&lt;/em&gt;, but most only know that there are a ton of programs and programmers on it. That&amp;rsquo;s not exactly wrong, but it&amp;rsquo;s not quite accurate either; and when I say I use &lt;em&gt;Git&lt;/em&gt;, some people conflate &lt;em&gt;Git&lt;/em&gt; with &lt;em&gt;GitHub&lt;/em&gt;; many think Git is very complicated, and by extension think GitHub is also very complicated&amp;hellip; So, I&amp;rsquo;d like to share my understanding of Git and GitHub, and talk about what both Git and GitHub actually are.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Syncing with rsync</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/shell_note/use_rsync/</link><pubDate>Mon, 28 Jul 2025 12:43:39 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/shell_note/use_rsync/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/%E8%B4%9D%E5%8A%A0%E5%B0%94%E6%B9%96%E7%95%94.jpg" alt="Featured image of post Syncing with rsync" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Kinda getting tired of &lt;code&gt;scp&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;sftp&lt;/code&gt;. Maybe I&amp;rsquo;ve just been using them wrong, but either way, I&amp;rsquo;m going with &lt;code&gt;rsync&lt;/code&gt;!&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;*&lt;del&gt;Couldn&amp;rsquo;t find the source for this image&amp;hellip; Snagged it from a friend &amp;ndash; it&amp;rsquo;s gorgeous, so I put it here.&lt;/del&gt; Found it! It&amp;rsquo;s by &lt;a class="link" href="https://www.pixiv.net/en/users/8605991" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;fasnakegod&lt;/a&gt; &amp;ndash; &lt;a class="link" href="https://www.pixiv.net/en/artworks/125739568" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;Lake Baikal&lt;/a&gt;. With that, let me share a piano piece. Here&amp;rsquo;s &lt;strong&gt;Kishi Ou no Hokori&lt;/strong&gt; (The Pride of the Knight King) for you all. (Totally unrelated, come on!) *&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>What Was the Last Command?</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/shell_note/last_command/</link><pubDate>Sat, 26 Jul 2025 20:09:18 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/shell_note/last_command/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/SEASIDE_SOLILOQUIES.png" alt="Featured image of post What Was the Last Command?" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I used to always wonder: how do you get the previous command? Should be simple, right? After a quick search, here&amp;rsquo;s what I found. Let me jot it down.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The header image is from Orangestar&amp;rsquo;s album &lt;strong&gt;SEASIDE SOLILOQUIES&lt;/strong&gt; &amp;ndash; beautiful art and great music. So for the track pick here, let&amp;rsquo;s go with the title song from that album: &lt;strong&gt;Alice in Reitouko&lt;/strong&gt; (Alice in the Freezer). Hope you enjoy it.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>C++ Vector Memory Layout</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/cpp_note/vector_memory_layout/</link><pubDate>Tue, 27 May 2025 09:53:29 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/cpp_note/vector_memory_layout/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/Bamboo_Reimu.jpg" alt="Featured image of post C++ Vector Memory Layout" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Exploring the memory layout of the C++ &lt;code&gt;vector&lt;/code&gt; container, and addressing some of my own doubts along the way&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The header image was found online. I tried to trace its source but had no luck — a real pity. For the music, I picked a relatively light and clear-sounding piece, &lt;strong&gt;Tears on Moss&lt;/strong&gt; (泪苔), which I feel matches the fresh, elegant atmosphere of the shrine in the header image. Hope you enjoy it.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Year of the Snake, Snake Lemma!</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/snake_lemma/</link><pubDate>Thu, 27 Feb 2025 00:00:00 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/snake_lemma/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/Post%20Shelter-Inaba%20Kumori.png" alt="Featured image of post Year of the Snake, Snake Lemma!" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I&amp;rsquo;ve watched so many videos proving the Snake Lemma, so I figured I&amp;rsquo;d give it a shot too~ It&amp;rsquo;s the Year of the Snake, participation matters, right&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
